      ":question/id": "b4e5d887-5a12-5211-a6c1-37fce679e237",
      ":question/problem": "Find the equation of the tangent to the curve $y={4}^{2x+1}$ at the point where $x=-1.$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "First, we express $y$ as the composite function\n\n$$\ny={4}^{u},\\,u=2x+1.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}={4}^{u}\\ln(4),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=2.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={4}^{u}\\ln(4)\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =2\\cdot {4}^{2x+1}\\ln(4).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to find the slope of the tangent to the curve at $x=-1$, we have to evaluate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ at that point. \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=-1} & ={(2\\cdot {4}^{2x+1}\\ln4)|}_{x=-1} \\\\  & =2\\cdot {4}^{-1}\\ln(4) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\ln(4) \\\\  & =\\ln((4)^{1/2}) \\\\  & =\\ln\\,(\\,\\sqrt{4}) \\\\  & =\\ln(2).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAlso, the $y$-value of the point on the curve where $x={x}_{1}=-1$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & ={({4}^{2x+1})|}_{x=-1} \\\\  & ={4}^{2(-1)+1} \\\\  & ={4}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we find the equation of the tangent at that point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-\\frac{1}{4} & =\\ln(2)\\cdot (x+1) \\\\ y-\\frac{1}{4} & =\\ln(2)\\,x+\\ln(2) \\\\ y & =\\ln(2)\\,x+\\ln(2)+\\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/hard",
